From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jan <1760229+slomin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 03:13:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] hot experts: the next layer's cold experts read ahead (#190)

At layer L the route op's threads score layer L+1's router against layer
L's routing input (an int8 copy of each router, built from the file), rank
the scores with L+1's residency bias, and thread 0 hands L+1's cold experts
among the top eight to three reader threads, which read them into side
buffers while L and L+1's attention compute. When L+1 admits an expert that
was read ahead, its repack takes the bytes from there. The policy and the
bytes are the same, so every output is too; only where an admission's bytes
come from changes.

On a Mac trace the next layer's float router catches half of its misses at
top 8 for about one read per miss. On the 12R 64% of admissions come from
the side buffers, 79-84% of those already complete; tg128 14.62 -> 15.29
(prefetch off/on, one build, two interleaved rounds). The reader threads
stay unpinned: kept off the compute cores they land on the little ones,
where only 31% of the reads were ready in time. LLAMA_HOT_PREFETCH=0 turns
it off, LLAMA_HOT_PREFETCH_N sets the rank cut.

